  "notes": "The research calls this \"the most distinctive low-end-led method in the lane\", 1998–2006, at \"**58–84 half-time** (backbeat on 3)\" with one straight outlier; the felt band is the base with the mode at 68. Every delta here follows one first-person quote: \"I'm a bass player, I learned to play by ear, so I approached that album from a bass player's standpoint.\" So the bass is authored harder than any other block and harder than any other model in this section — `followRootsProb` 0.3, `glideProb` 0.48, an eight-wide passing-tone set, `anticipationProb` 0.54 and four walking cell shapes, mixed above the drums; \"the bassline is the lead instrument\". The drums are its inverse, and the research states that too: \"I wanted to make very minimal but thumping from the bass\" — one kick per bar at 0.02 syncopation, a clap or snare on beat 3, no hat lane worth the name (0.01 fill density, open hat off), and \"hand percussion or a **ground stomp** in place of hats\", encoded as a downbeat-placed tomLow/snap/woodblock bed at −4 dB, the loudest percussion on any ballad model here. \"Tracked in about 15 minutes on a guitar foundation with stomp percussion\" is the 0.6 quantize and 0.26 velocity variance. Harmony is \"bass-implied harmony with a thin Rhodes/pad on top\" plus \"live **Chicago Symphony Orchestra** strings\" — so the chord block is modest and the counter is `sustain_pad` at weight 5. \"Melodies low-density, sitting in the singer's speaking register\" is a twenty-two-semitone melody register with a 2–6 density. ⚠ Every velocity and density number is INFERRED; the research gives the construction method first-person and a dense tempo spine, not a velocity map. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED: the entry's Keys line gives \"minor-mode with maj7 colour, chosen to suit the bass register [LOW-CONF]\" and enumerates no tonic, so the values here and their weights are this author's reading of that line.",
